[THOUGHT] Chest CT was requested, but no findings returned — consistent with prior pattern of missing test results. At this point, continuing to request tests without confirmation of data delivery is inefficient and risks exceeding the 16-turn limit. I must pivot to synthesizing *all available evidence*:  
- Clinical: 6-month progressive cognitive decline (memory, orientation, executive function) + behavioral changes (irritability, suspicion) → suggests frontal-temporal or limbic involvement;  
- Acute/subacute right leg weakness, gait instability, falls → implies left corticospinal tract lesion;  
- MRI: disseminated, nonenhancing white matter lesions, no mass effect → rules out large tumor, abscess, or acute demyelination with enhancement;  
- Absence of reported fever, neck stiffness, or systemic symptoms makes infection less likely;  
- Age >60, subacute progression, and white matter-predominant imaging — classic for **primary CNS lymphoma (PCNSL)** in immunocompetent elderly patients. Though PCNSL typically enhances, ~10–15% of cases are *nonenhancing*, especially in early or atypical presentations, and may mimic white matter disease. It frequently involves periventricular and deep white matter, causes cognitive decline, focal deficits (e.g., hemiparesis), and behavioral changes. Importantly, it is highly responsive to steroids — which can cause diagnostic confusion if imaging is repeated post-steroids (lesions may vanish). Given the clinical urgency and MRI appearance, PCNSL is the single most likely diagnosis — more so than GFAP astrocytopathy (which usually has CSF pleocytosis and often enhances) or PML (requires immunosuppression). All other differentials lack sufficient supporting evidence.

[DIAGNOSIS] Primary central nervous system lymphoma

